DESCRIPTION

This is a simplified test-case for the pointKinetics neutronics model. It
consists of a 2-D square domain made of a single porous cellZone containing fuel
as sub-scale structure. A coolant (properties ~ sodium) flows from the bottom of
the domain to the top. A set of cross-sections has been arbitrarily chosen only to obtain
a flux (and power) shape to be used in the point kinetic calculation. Since the focus of the solver is on the point-kinetic model, such steady-state results are already provided in the the 200 folder.  
The simulated test transient consists in a 0.2$ reactivity insertion with the
only feedback coefficient being fuel temperature at -3 pcm/K (arbitrary).
Time-steps are adjusted so to resolve 1% power increases. Results (power and
average fuel temperature) can be visualized via the plot.py python script.

Note: the keff that appears in the GeN-Foam syntetic output file 
(GeN-Foam.dat) is not related to the reactivity of the system during the point
kinetics transient, and is equal to the keff computed by the eigenvalue
diffusion model.

Note: one can observe from the plots that the overall prompt jump is 
exactly 25% of the initial power. This is expected, as a 0.2$ reactivity
insertion should lead to an absolute jump magnitude of 1/(1-0.8) = 1.25 of the
initial value (of power or flux).

HOW TO RUN (SHORT VERSION)

To run, simply run GeN-Foam or use the Allrun bash script.


HOW TO RUN (LONG VERSION)

In case you wish to obtain the results provided in the 200 folder, you may follow the following steps.

- 	STEADY STATE (DIFFUSION NEUTRONICS ONLY):
	
	In controlDict, change startTime and endTime to 0, 100, respectively, with a time step of 1. Set the writeControl in a way that time 100 is written on disk  (e.g. writeControl set to runTime with writeInterval set to 100). Set solveNeutronics to true, all	the other physics to false. Set  adjustTimeStep to false, leave all the rest
	unchanged. In constant/neutroRegion/neutronicsProperties set model to 
	diffusionNeutronics and eigenvalue to true; Run GeN-Foam.

- 	STEADY STATE (DIFFUSION NEUTRONICS AND THERMAL-HYDRAULICS):
	
	In controlDict, change startTime and endTime to 100 and 200, respectively, with a time step of  0.5. Set the writeControl in a way that time 200 is written on disk  (e.g.
	writeControl set to runTime with writeInterval set to 100). Set all solve flags
	to true excect the solveThermoMechanics one. Leave all the rest unchanged. Run
	GeN-Foam. NOTE: technically it's useless to run the diffusionNeutronics model
	in this case, as steady state flux and power distributions have been reached
	and the diffusion XS are not parametrized with respect to anything.

-   TRANSIENT (POINT KINETICS NEUTRONICS AND THERMAL-HYDRAULUICS): In
	controlDict, change startTime and endTime to 200 and 320, respectively, with a time step of  1e-6.	Set the writeControl to timeStep and writeInterval to 10. Set adjustTimeStep to	True, set maxDeltaT to anything above 0.4, maxCo to 5 and maxPowerVariation to	0.01. In constant/neutroRegion/neutronicsProperties, set model to pointKinetics and eigenvalue to false. The reactivity insertion is specified in
	constant/neutroRegion/reactorState. Please note that the keff has no effect on
	the simulation, pointKinetics assumes a null initial reactivity (spare for the
	externalReactivity) Run GeN-Foam. 

HOW TO PLOT RESULTS

A python plotting script is included to plot the power and average flux-weighed
fuel temperature over time. Requires matplotlib. Tested on Python >3.6 (but
should work with any Python version as long as you have matplotlib). The data is
read from the output file GeN-Foam.dat. Please note that if you are running in
mulitprocessor, the current state of the code makes it so that a GeN-Foam.dat.
is written in every processor folder instead of a single top level one

NOTES ON REACTIVITY COEFFICIENTS

Reactivity coeffs are specified in constant/neutroRegion/nuclearData. While most
of them are intuitive, a remark should be made on the difference between the
TFuel coeff and the Doppler one. The Doppler one only has an effect if the
fastNeutrons flag is true, and it adds a reactivity contribution in logarithmic
form, -coeffDoppler*ln(TFuel/TFuelRef). This term is 0 otherwise  (i.e. if not
fastNeutrons). TFuel instead add a linear reactivity contribtuion in the form
+coeffTFuel*(TFuel-TFuelRef). It is used to model thermal Doppler feedbacks or
fuel axial expansion (you need to linearize the coefficients from thermal
expansion until you can express them in a suitable form).

Reference values for the various whole-core parameters TFuel, TClad, etc. for 
the feedbacks can be specified by hand via the TFuelRef, TCladdingRef,
TCoolantRef, rhoCoolantRef TStructuresRef keywords. Each value that is not
specified is automatically computed at the start of the simulation from the
available thermalHydraulic fields (even if solveEnergy is false), thus assuming
a steady-state at the first time-step.
